{ignore}
<p>The body between <code>&#123;define 'card'&#125;</code> and <code>&#123;/define&#125;</code>
becomes the template of a new tag, <code>&#123;card&#125;</code>, exactly as if it stood in
<code>_tags/card.pad</code>. Defining prints nothing; every use of the tag renders the body,
where the use stands and with the fields there.</p>

<p>Everything a file-based custom tag can do works in the body: <code>&#123;#name&#125;</code>
reads a parameter of the use, <a href="?tag/parms"><code>&#123;parms&#125;</code></a> declares
them (a bare name required, <code>name=default</code> filling in),
<code>&#64;content&#64;</code> takes the content of a pair use in, and
<a href="?tag/slot"><code>&#123;slot&#125;</code></a>s take named fills. A define can use
itself, for rows below the current one.</p>

<p>The body is kept as written and rendered at each use, so a field in it is read at the use,
not at the definition. A define exists from the point it is walked - like a
<code>&#123;content&#125;</code> or <code>&#123;data&#125;</code> store - so a use above the
<code>&#123;define&#125;</code> is an unknown tag. <code>&#123;define:card&#125;</code> asserts
that the name is a define.</p>

<p>Under the strict check a name that is a built-in or application tag, or already a
<a href="?tag/macro"><code>&#123;macro&#125;</code></a>, is an error, and so is a define
without a name or without a body. Use a define for markup with a frame - content and slots;
use a macro when the parameters should read as plain fields.</p>
{/ignore}
